than that in same-sign pairs, and is stronger in the out-of-plane (\(UD\)) than in-plane (\(LR\)) direction regardless of charge combinations. From mid-central to central collisions, the same-sign pairs are preferentially back-to-back, while the opposite-sign pairs are still preferentially aligned in the same direction and more so than in peripheral collisions. The opposite-sign pair small angle correlation is always stronger in the out-of-plane (\(UD\)) than in-plane (\(LR\)) direction. On the other hand, the back-to-back emission tendency of same-sign pairs is weaker in the out-of-plane (\(UD\)) that in-plane (\(LR\)) direction. It is interesting to see that same-sign pair correlations show different behavior in peripheral collisions and most central collisions, which implies different mechanisms dominate in each situation.

